---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import PlatformSwitch from "../components/PlatformSwitch.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
import QuadrantScatter from "../components/charts/QuadrantScatter.astro";
import RatioSwarm from "../components/charts/RatioSwarm.astro";
import LoadArrows from "../components/charts/LoadArrows.astro";
import MemoryDumbbell from "../components/charts/MemoryDumbbell.astro";
import ScaleBars from "../components/charts/ScaleBars.astro";
import { platforms, loadRun, scripted, fmtOps, fmtX, fmtMiB, pct, scale, scaleDeep, scaleEverysec, shardScaling } from "../data/bench";
import { site } from "../config/site";
import { clamp } from "../lib/geom";

const [mac, azure] = platforms;
const blob = (f: string) => `${site.github}/blob/main/${f}`;
const popular = ["GET", "SET", "DEL (missing key)", "SETEX", "HSET (existing field)", "HGET", "LPUSH", "ZADD (existing member, same score)", "EXPIRE (key with TTL)", "PUBLISH (no subscribers)"];
const peakNames = ["GET", "SET", "DEL (missing key)", "SETEX", "HGET", "HSET (existing field)", "LPUSH"];
const peakGet = scaleDeep.row("GET"), peakSet = scaleDeep.row("SET"), peakDel = scaleDeep.row("DEL (missing key)");
const peakHeadline = Math.round(Math.min(peakGet.ruvio, peakSet.ruvio) / 1e6);
const mil = (v: number) => (v / 1e6).toFixed(1);
const grids = [
  { name: `In-memory, pipeline ${scale.pipeline}`, s: scale },
  { name: `In-memory, pipeline ${scaleDeep.pipeline}`, s: scaleDeep },
  { name: `everysec against Redis AOF everysec`, s: scaleEverysec },
];

// Ratio bar in the table: log2 scale from 0.5× to 2.5×, centred on Redis = 1×.
const LO = 0.5, HI = 2.5;
const at = (v: number) => (Math.log2(clamp(v, LO, HI) / LO) / Math.log2(HI / LO)) * 100;
const one = at(1);
---

<Page title="Benchmarks" description="Ruvio against Redis 8.10.2 on the same machines: throughput for 60 commands, memory for 12 data shapes, and p99 latency under load. Every number comes from a record in the repository.">
  <PageIntro title="Measured against Redis, on the same machine, from the records in the repository" kicker="Benchmarks">
    Each run starts both servers on one host, drives them with the same load generator in alternating rounds, and
    stores the raw numbers in a JSON file next to the site. The charts below are drawn from those files when the
    site is built. Runs of hours under load and failures are on the <a href="/testing/">long-run tests</a> page.
    <div class="kpis two" slot="after">
      {platforms.map((p) => (
        <div>
          <span class="plat">{p.name}</span>
          <b>{fmtX(p.geomean)}</b>
          <span>geometric mean over {p.rows.length} commands; {p.atPar} at 0.97× or faster; memory −{pct(p.memSaved)}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </PageIntro>

  <section class="section" id="scripted">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head-row">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">One command instead of a script</p>
          <h2 class="title">{fmtX(Math.min(...scripted.rows.map((r) => r.ratio)))} to {fmtX(Math.max(...scripted.rows.map((r) => r.ratio)))} the throughput of the same job written as a Lua script</h2>
          <p class="lede">Moving a balance, popping due jobs, a sliding-window limiter or a counter that gets a deadline when it is created are one command in Ruvio. On Redis the usual answer is a Lua script. Each row runs the Ruvio command against that script (<code>EVALSHA</code>) on the same machine. The table compares the two ways of doing the job, not the servers in general.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll-x">
        <table class="res">
          <thead><tr><th>Ruvio command</th><th class="num">Ruvio ops/s</th><th class="num">Redis Lua ops/s</th><th class="num">Ratio</th><th class="num">p50 ms</th><th class="num">p99 ms</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {scripted.rows.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{r.label}</td>
                <td class="num">{fmtOps(r.ruvio)}</td>
                <td class="num dim">{fmtOps(r.redis)}</td>
                <td class="num">{fmtX(r.ratio)}</td>
                <td class="num dim">{r.ruvioP50.toFixed(2)} / {r.redisP50.toFixed(2)}</td>
                <td class="num dim">{r.ruvioP99.toFixed(2)} / {r.redisP99.toFixed(2)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll-x scale-table">
        <table class="res">
          <thead><tr><th>Memory per key</th><th class="num">Ruvio</th><th class="num">Redis</th><th class="num">Ratio</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {scripted.memory.map((m) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{m.label}</td>
                <td class="num">{Math.round(m.ruvioBytes)} B</td>
                <td class="num dim">{Math.round(m.redisBytes)} B</td>
                <td class="num">{fmtX(m.ratio)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="source">Source: <a href={blob(scripted.file)}>{scripted.file}</a> · {scripted.machine}, {scripted.date}, commit {scripted.commit} · Redis {scripted.redisVersion} · {scripted.clients} clients, pipeline {scripted.pipeline}, {scripted.rounds} alternating rounds, median · p50 and p99 are Ruvio / Redis with one request in flight per connection · in-memory, 1 shard · macOS only, not yet measured on Linux or x86 · semaphore memory is compared with a one-member sorted set, Redis's most compact structure for the job.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band" id="machines">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head-row">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Two machines</p>
          <h2 class="title">Where each command lands on both machines</h2>
          <p class="lede">Each dot is one command: its ratio to Redis on {mac.name} across, on {azure.name} up. The shaded corner holds the commands that are faster than Redis on both. Dots near the faint diagonal behave the same on ARM and x86.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="quad-grid">
        <div class="chart" data-chart><QuadrantScatter x={mac} y={azure} /></div>
        <div class="notes">
          <h3>Reading it</h3>
          <p>The two machines are very different: a 12-core ARM laptop with no pinning, and an x86 cloud VM with the server and the load generator pinned to separate cores. A command that stays top-right on both is fast because of how Ruvio runs it, not because of the hardware.</p>
          <div class="legend">
            <span><i style="background:var(--c1)"></i>faster on both</span>
            <span><i style="background:var(--c2)"></i>faster on one</span>
            <span><i style="background:var(--redis)"></i>behind on both</span>
          </div>
          <p class="source">Sources: <a href={blob(mac.file)}>{mac.file}</a>, <a href={blob(azure.file)}>{azure.file}</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="spread">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head-row">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Distribution</p>
          <h2 class="title">All {mac.rows.length} commands on one axis</h2>
          <p class="lede">The same results as a swarm: one dot per command, stacked where they crowd. Most of the mass sits right of the dashed Redis line on both machines.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll-x"><div class="chart swarm-box" data-chart><RatioSwarm platforms={platforms} /></div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band" id="load">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head-row">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Under load</p>
          <h2 class="title">Throughput and tail latency together</h2>
          <p class="lede">Each line joins Redis (hollow) to Ruvio (filled). In the first column further right is more requests per second; in the second, further left is a lower p99. Rows cover client counts, pipelining, fsync on every write, and more Ruvio shards.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="legend"><span><i class="o"></i>Redis</span><span><i style="background:var(--c1)"></i>Ruvio</span></div>
      </div>
      <LoadArrows />
      <p class="source">Source: <a href={blob(loadRun.file)}>{loadRun.file}</a> · {loadRun.machine} · {loadRun.date}, commit {loadRun.commit} · server and load generator share the VM, so compare the two servers with each other, not with other hosts. Un-pipelined rows are limited by the load generator.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band" id="peak">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head-row">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Peak throughput</p>
          <h2 class="title">{peakHeadline}M requests per second on one machine</h2>
          <p class="lede">With {scaleDeep.shards} shards on {scaleDeep.shards} cores and a client that keeps them busy (pipeline {scaleDeep.pipeline}), the simplest commands reach about {peakHeadline} million requests per second: <code>GET</code> {mil(peakGet.ruvio)}M, <code>SET</code> {mil(peakSet.ruvio)}M, <code>DEL</code> {mil(peakDel.ruvio)}M.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpis">
        <div><span class="plat">GET</span><b>{mil(peakGet.ruvio)}M</b><span>requests per second on {scaleDeep.shards} shards; one Redis process on one core serves {mil(peakGet.redis1)}M, a {scaleDeep.shards}-node Redis Cluster {mil(peakGet.cluster)}M</span></div>
        <div><span class="plat">SET</span><b>{mil(peakSet.ruvio)}M</b><span>requests per second; the cluster reaches {mil(peakSet.cluster)}M ({fmtX(peakSet.vsCluster)} for Ruvio)</span></div>
        <div><span class="plat">Per shard</span><b>{mil(peakGet.ruvio / scaleDeep.shards)}M</b><span>GET requests per second for each shard, one core each; {scaleDeep.shards} shards give {mil(peakGet.ruvio)}M because they never wait for one another</span></div>
        <div><span class="plat">Why it is fast</span><b>0<em> locks</em></b><span>each shard owns its keys on its own core; a command never waits for another shard, so shards add up</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="scale-chart"><ScaleBars scale={scaleDeep} names={peakNames} /></div>
      <div class="peak-notes">
        <div><h3>What the number is</h3><p>The sum over {scaleDeep.shards} shards, each driven by its own <code>redis-benchmark</code> ({scaleDeep.clients} clients, pipeline {scaleDeep.pipeline}, in-memory). It shows what the hot path can do when the network and the client are not the limit.</p></div>
        <div><h3>What it is not</h3><p>Not every command: heavier ones such as <code>SETEX</code>, <code>HSET</code> or <code>LPUSH</code> run at {mil(Math.min(...peakNames.slice(3).map((n) => scaleDeep.row(n).ruvio)))}M to {mil(Math.max(...peakNames.slice(3).map((n) => scaleDeep.row(n).ruvio)))}M, and large replies are limited by the network. Without pipelining, a request waits for its reply and the load generator becomes the limit.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Conditions</h3><p>One Azure VM, F16as_v6 (AMD EPYC 9V74, virtualized, 16 cores, no SMT): servers on cpus 8-15, load generators on cpus 0-7, loopback, {scaleDeep.date}. Server and load generator share the VM, so compare the systems with each other, not with other hosts.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="source">Source: <a href={blob(scaleDeep.file)}>{scaleDeep.file}</a> · the same run as the pipeline {scaleDeep.pipeline} grid below.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="scale">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head-row">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Whole machine</p>
          <h2 class="title">{scale.shards} shards against a Redis Cluster of {scale.shards} and a single Redis</h2>
          <p class="lede">One <code>redis-benchmark</code> per shard, pinned to its own core, sends each command to that shard's port with the keys inside a hash tag the shard owns, so nothing crosses shards. The same load goes to each node of a Redis Cluster on the same {scale.shards} cores, and to one Redis process on one core. Rates are summed over the shards.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpis">
        {grids.map((g) => (
          <div><span class="plat">{g.name}</span><b>{fmtX(g.s.geomeanCluster)}</b><span>geometric mean against the cluster over {g.s.rows.length - 1} commands; {g.s.atParCluster} of {g.s.rows.length} at 0.97× or faster; per core {fmtX(g.s.geomeanPerCore)} of one Redis</span></div>
        ))}
        <div><span class="plat">Memory</span><b>{fmtX(scale.memGeomeanPerShard)}</b><span>per shard against one Redis over {scale.memory.length} data shapes ({fmtX(scale.memGeomeanCluster)} against the cluster's total)</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="scale-chart"><ScaleBars scale={scale} names={popular} /></div>
      <div class="table-wrap scale-table">
        <table class="res">
          <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th class="num">Ruvio {scale.shards} shards</th><th class="num">Redis Cluster {scale.shards}</th><th class="num">One Redis</th><th class="num">vs cluster</th><th class="num">per core vs one Redis</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {[...scale.rows].sort((a, b) => b.vsCluster - a.vsCluster).map((r) => {
              const k = r.vsCluster >= 1 ? "up" : r.vsCluster >= 0.97 ? "par" : "down";
              return (
                <tr><td>{r.label}</td><td class="num">{fmtOps(r.ruvio)}</td><td class="num dim">{fmtOps(r.cluster)}</td><td class="num dim">{fmtOps(r.redis1)}</td><td class={`num x ${k}`}>{fmtX(r.vsCluster)}</td><td class="num dim">{fmtX(r.perCore)}</td></tr>
              );
            })}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap scale-table">
        <table class="res">
          <thead><tr><th>Data shape ({scale.shards} copies on the {scale.shards}-shard systems)</th><th class="num">Ruvio {scale.shards} shards</th><th class="num">Redis Cluster {scale.shards}</th><th class="num">One Redis, one copy</th><th class="num">per shard vs one Redis</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {scale.memory.map((m) => (
              <tr><td>{m.label}</td><td class="num">{fmtMiB(m.ruvioKib)} MiB</td><td class="num dim">{fmtMiB(m.clusterKib)} MiB</td><td class="num dim">{fmtMiB(m.redis1Kib)} MiB</td><td class={`num x ${m.perShardVsOne <= 1 ? "up" : "down"}`}>{fmtX(m.perShardVsOne)}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="source">Sources: {grids.map((g, i) => <span>{i ? " · " : ""}<a href={blob(g.s.file)}>{g.s.file}</a></span>)} · Azure VM F16as_v6 (AMD EPYC 9V74, virtualized), 16 cores without SMT; servers on cpus 8-15, load generators on cpus 0-7, loopback · {scale.date}, Ruvio {scale.commit} · the record with the one-shard control run and the profiles is <a href="/docs/evidence/2026-10-16-azure-8-shards/">in the docs</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="shard-scaling">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head-row">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">More shards</p>
          <h2 class="title">Adding shards with a cluster-aware client</h2>
          <p class="lede">Also on the <a href="/testing/">long-run tests</a> page. One <code>memtier_benchmark</code> (8 threads, pipeline {shardScaling.pipeline}) routes each key to the shard that owns it: <code>--cluster-mode</code> against Ruvio, and against a Redis Cluster with one master per core (one plain Redis for one shard). <code>SET</code> and <code>GET</code> over 100,000 keys, median of {shardScaling.rounds} rounds.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap scale-table">
        <table class="res">
          <thead><tr><th>Shards</th><th class="num">Ruvio SET</th><th class="num">Redis SET</th><th class="num">Ratio</th><th class="num">Ruvio GET</th><th class="num">Redis GET</th><th class="num">Ratio</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {shardScaling.routed.map((r) => {
              const s = r.ruvio.set / r.redis.set, g = r.ruvio.get / r.redis.get;
              return (
                <tr><td>{r.shards}</td><td class="num">{fmtOps(r.ruvio.set)}</td><td class="num dim">{fmtOps(r.redis.set)}</td><td class={`num x ${s >= 1 ? "up" : s >= 0.97 ? "par" : "down"}`}>{fmtX(s)}</td><td class="num">{fmtOps(r.ruvio.get)}</td><td class="num dim">{fmtOps(r.redis.get)}</td><td class={`num x ${g >= 1 ? "up" : g >= 0.97 ? "par" : "down"}`}>{fmtX(g)}</td></tr>
              );
            })}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>One port, a client that does not route</h3>
      <p class="lede">Every connection on the first port; the server forwards each command to the shard that owns its key. Since the forwarding shard wakes each other shard once per turn instead of once per send:</p>
      <div class="table-wrap scale-table">
        <table class="res">
          <thead><tr><th>Shards</th><th class="num">SET before</th><th class="num">SET now</th><th class="num">GET before</th><th class="num">GET now</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {shardScaling.onePort.map((r) => (
              <tr><td>{r.shards}</td><td class="num dim">{fmtOps(r.before.set)}</td><td class="num">{fmtOps(r.after.set)}</td><td class="num dim">{fmtOps(r.before.get)}</td><td class="num">{fmtOps(r.after.get)}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>One shard, same run</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap scale-table">
        <table class="res">
          <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th class="num">Ruvio</th><th class="num">Redis {shardScaling.redisVersion}</th><th class="num">Ratio</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {shardScaling.oneShard.map((r) => (
              <tr><td>{r.label}</td><td class="num">{fmtOps(r.ruvio)}</td><td class="num dim">{fmtOps(r.redis)}</td><td class={`num x ${r.ratio >= 1 ? "up" : r.ratio >= 0.97 ? "par" : "down"}`}>{fmtX(r.ratio)}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="source">Source: <a href={blob(shardScaling.file)}>{shardScaling.file}</a> · {shardScaling.machine}; servers on cpus 8-15, load on cpus 0-7, loopback · {shardScaling.date}, Ruvio {shardScaling.commit} · 8 shards were measured too but are not shown: eight load threads were the limit for both systems there · <a href="/docs/evidence/2026-10-26-azure-shard-scaling/">record in the docs</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div data-platform-scope>
    <section class="section band" id="results">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head-row">
          <div>
            <p class="kicker">Every row</p>
            <h2 class="title">Full results</h2>
            <p class="lede">Median requests per second over the rounds, Ruvio and Redis side by side. The whisker spans the worst and best pairing of rounds, so a wide whisker means a noisy row.</p>
          </div>
          <PlatformSwitch />
        </div>
        {platforms.map((p, i) => (
          <div data-platform={p.id} hidden={i !== 0}>
            <div class="chart" data-chart><div class="table-wrap">
              <table class="res">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Command</th><th class="num">Ruvio ops/s</th><th class="num">Redis ops/s</th><th class="bar-h"><div class="bt"><span>0.5×</span><span style={`left:${one}%`}>1×</span><span>2.5×</span></div></th><th class="num">Ratio</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {[...p.rows].sort((a, b) => b.ratio - a.ratio).map((r) => {
                    const k = r.ratio >= 1 ? "up" : r.ratio >= 0.97 ? "par" : "down";
                    const x = at(r.ratio);
                    return (
                      <tr tabindex="0" data-tip={r.label} data-tip-sub={`${fmtX(r.ratio)} Redis`} data-tip-sub2={`rounds: ${fmtX(r.ratioLo)} to ${fmtX(r.ratioHi)}`}>
                        <td>{r.label}</td>
                        <td class="num">{fmtOps(r.ruvio)}</td>
                        <td class="num dim">{fmtOps(r.redis)}</td>
                        <td class="bar"><div class="bt">
                          <span class="ax" style={`left:${one}%`}></span>
                          <span class="wh" style={`left:${at(r.ratioLo)}%;width:${at(r.ratioHi) - at(r.ratioLo)}%`}></span>
                          <span class={`b ${k}`} style={`left:${Math.min(x, one)}%;width:${Math.abs(x - one)}%`}></span>
                        </div></td>
                        <td class={`num x ${k}`}>{fmtX(r.ratio)}</td>
                      </tr>
                    );
                  })}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div></div>
            <p class="source">Source: <a href={blob(p.file)}>{p.file}</a> · {p.date}, commit {p.commit}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="memory">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head-row">
          <div>
            <p class="kicker">Memory</p>
            <h2 class="title">Memory added per data shape</h2>
            <p class="lede">Resident memory after loading minus resident memory at idle, for each server. Left is less, on a log axis.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="legend"><span><i style="background:var(--c1)"></i>Ruvio</span><span><i style="background:var(--redis)"></i>Redis</span></div>
        </div>
        {platforms.map((p, i) => (
          <div data-platform={p.id} hidden={i !== 0}>
            <div class="chart" data-chart><MemoryDumbbell platform={p} /></div>
            <p class="source">{p.memLower} of {p.memory.length} shapes below Redis · total {fmtMiB(p.memory.reduce((a, m) => a + m.ruvioKib, 0))} MiB against {fmtMiB(p.memory.reduce((a, m) => a + m.redisKib, 0))} MiB · <a href={blob(p.file)}>{p.file}</a></p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>


  <section class="section band" id="method">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head-row">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Method</p>
          <h2 class="title">How the numbers are made</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="method">
        {platforms.map((p) => (
          <dl>
            <dt class="h">{p.name}</dt>
            <dt>Machine</dt><dd>{p.machine}</dd>
            <dt>Redis</dt><dd>{p.redisVersion}</dd>
            <dt>Load</dt><dd>{p.clients} clients, pipeline {p.pipeline}, {p.rounds} alternating rounds, median</dd>
            <dt>Ruvio</dt><dd>{p.durability} profile, {p.shards} shard, release build, commit {p.commit}</dd>
            <dt>Pinning</dt><dd>{p.pinning.replace(/\s*\(.*\)$/, "")}</dd>
          </dl>
        ))}
        <div class="how">
          <p>The whole-machine grids come from <code>scripts/shard_compare.py</code>: {scale.shards} shards in one Ruvio process (<code>port_mode = "per-shard"</code>), a Redis Cluster of {scale.shards} masters created with <code>redis-cli --cluster create</code>, and one Redis process, each driven by one <code>redis-benchmark</code> per shard with hash-tagged keys. The {scale.shards}-shard systems hold {scale.shards} copies of each data shape, so a shard holds what the single process holds.</p>
          <p>The single-machine rows come from <code>scripts/redis_compare_features.py</code>, which runs <code>redis-benchmark</code> against each server in turn. Absolute numbers describe one host; the ratio between the two servers is the result. Shared cloud machines vary from run to run, so a row within a few percent of 1× is a tie.</p>
          <p><a class="btn ghost small" href="/docs/benchmarking/">Benchmarking guide</a> <a class="btn ghost small" href={`${site.github}/tree/main/benchmark/records`}>All records</a> <a class="btn ghost small" href="/testing/">Long-run tests</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
